/-- Canonical Bregman divergence generated by a differentiable potential `v`:
`D_v(x,z) = v z - v x - ⟪∇ v x, z - x⟫`.
Layer: Model | Concept: Bregman
Proof: (definitional construction; canonical three-term Bregman formula)
Source: Lan, First-order and Stochastic Optimization Methods for Machine Learning,
  Eq. (3.2.2).
Used in: stochastic mirror descent Bregman geometry
Book citation: `book/FOML/StochasticMirrorDescent.json#/assumptions/5/math`
Origin algorithm: FOML stochastic mirror descent -/
noncomputable def bregmanDivergence
    {E : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup E] [InnerProductSpace ℝ E] [CompleteSpace E]
    (v : E → ℝ) (x z : E) : ℝ :=
  v z - v x - ⟪∇ v x, z - x⟫_ℝ

/-- Literal interior-base Bregman divergence for mirror-descent geometry.

This construction packages the three-term Bregman formula using a potential on the
carrier space and an ambient differentiable representative to supply the
interior gradient term.

Layer: Model | Concept: Bregman
Proof: (definitional construction; literal Bregman divergence wrapper with an
  interior-base gradient correction term)
Source: Mathlib inner product spaces and Fréchet-derivative gradient notation
Used in: stochastic mirror descent prox objective and Bregman geometry setup
Book citation: book/FOML/StochasticMirrorDescent.json#/algorithm_spec
Origin algorithm: Lan, First-order and Stochastic Optimization Methods for Machine Learning, stochastic mirror descent -/
noncomputable def literalBregmanDivergence
    {E Xint P : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup E] [InnerProductSpace ℝ E] [CompleteSpace E]
    (v : P → ℝ) (vInterior : E → ℝ) (toPoint : Xint → P)
    (evalInterior : Xint → E) (eval : P → E) (x : Xint) (z : P) : ℝ :=
  v z - v (toPoint x) - ⟪∇ vInterior (evalInterior x), eval z - evalInterior x⟫_ℝ

/-- Expanded literal formula for the interior-base Bregman divergence.

The model-level Bregman wrapper unfolds to `v z - v (toPoint x)` minus the
gradient linearization term at the interior representative of `x`.

Layer: Model | Gap: Level 0 (literal interior Bregman formula expansion)
Proof: by rfl after unfolding literalBregmanDivergence.
Source: Mathlib inner product space and gradient notation APIs
Used in: stochastic mirror descent prox-step algebra and literal Bregman formula audits
Book citation: book/FOML/StochasticMirrorDescent.json#/algorithm_spec
Origin algorithm: Lan, First-order and Stochastic Optimization Methods for Machine Learning, stochastic mirror descent -/
theorem literalBregmanDivergence_def
    {E Xint P : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup E] [InnerProductSpace ℝ E] [CompleteSpace E]
    (v : P → ℝ) (vInterior : E → ℝ) (toPoint : Xint → P)
    (evalInterior : Xint → E) (eval : P → E) (x : Xint) (z : P) :
    literalBregmanDivergence v vInterior toPoint evalInterior eval x z =
      v z - v (toPoint x) - ⟪∇ vInterior (evalInterior x), eval z - evalInterior x⟫_ℝ := by
  rfl

/-- The literal Bregman formula for a carrier potential at topological-interior base points.

This packages the expansion of an interior ambient-gradient Bregman expression
for a potential defined on a feasible carrier, with the base point coerced from
`interior X` back to the carrier `X`.

Layer: Model | Gap: Level 1 (literal carrier Bregman formula expansion)
Proof: specialize an abstract definitional expansion hypothesis for the carrier
  Bregman value at the requested interior base point and feasible endpoint.
Source: Mathlib inner-product space and gradient notation APIs
Used in: stochastic mirror descent interior-base Bregman formula bridge
Book citation: book/FOML/StochasticMirrorDescent.json#/algorithm_spec/parameters/2/math
Origin algorithm: Lan, First-order and Stochastic Optimization Methods for
  Machine Learning, stochastic mirror descent -/
theorem literalCarrierBregmanDivergence
    {E : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup E] [InnerProductSpace ℝ E] [CompleteSpace E]
    {X : Set E} (v : {x : E // x ∈ X} → ℝ) (vInterior : E → ℝ)
    (V : {x : E // x ∈ interior X} → {x : E // x ∈ X} → ℝ)
    (hV : ∀ x z,
      V x z =
        v z - v ⟨x.1, interior_subset x.2⟩ -
          ⟪∇ vInterior x.1, z.1 - x.1⟫_ℝ)
    (x : {x : E // x ∈ interior X}) (z : {x : E // x ∈ X}) :
    V x z =
      v z - v ⟨x.1, interior_subset x.2⟩ -
        ⟪∇ vInterior x.1, z.1 - x.1⟫_ℝ := by
  exact hV x z



/-- The Bregman three-point identity for the canonical divergence
`D_v(x,z) = v z - v x - ⟪∇ v x, z - x⟫`.
Layer: Layer0 | Gap: Level 1 (Bregman algebra identity)
Proof: Expand the definition, split `z - x` through the intermediate point `y`,
  and normalize the resulting inner-product algebra.
Source: Lan, First-order and Stochastic Optimization Methods for Machine Learning,
  Eq. (3.2.6).
Used in: `bregman_three_point_identity`, stochastic mirror descent prox inequality
Book citation: `book/FOML/StochasticMirrorDescent.json#/assumptions/5/math`
Origin algorithm: FOML stochastic mirror descent -/
theorem bregmanDivergence_three_point_identity
    {E : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup E] [InnerProductSpace ℝ E] [CompleteSpace E]
    (v : E → ℝ) (x y z : E) :
    bregmanDivergence v x z =
      bregmanDivergence v x y + ⟪∇ v y - ∇ v x, z - y⟫_ℝ +
        bregmanDivergence v y z := by
  unfold bregmanDivergence
  have hsplit : z - x = (z - y) + (y - x) := by
    abel
  rw [hsplit, inner_add_right, inner_sub_left]
  ring



/-- Strong convexity on an intrinsic interior gives the usual Bregman lower bound.
Layer: Layer0 | Gap: Level 2 (relative-interior Bregman lower bound)
Proof: Restrict the convexified function `v - (1 / 2)‖·‖²` to the segment
  from `x` to `z`, compare its one-sided derivative at `0` to the secant slope,
  and expand the squared norm identity.
Source: Lan, First-order and Stochastic Optimization Methods for Machine Learning,
  Proposition 3.1 / Bregman divergence lower bound.
Used in: `MirrorDescentSetup.V_lower_bound_of_intrinsicInterior_core`
Book citation: `book/FOML/StochasticMirrorDescent.json#/assumptions/5/math`
Origin algorithm: FOML stochastic mirror descent -/
theorem bregman_lower_bound_of_strongConvexOn_intrinsicInterior
    {E : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup E] [InnerProductSpace ℝ E] [CompleteSpace E]
    {X : Set E} {v : E → ℝ} {grad : E → E} {V : E → E → ℝ} {x z : E}
    (hV : V x z = v z - v x - ⟪grad x, z - x⟫_ℝ)
    (h_strong : StrongConvexOn (intrinsicInterior ℝ X) 1 v)
    (h_diff : DifferentiableOn ℝ v (intrinsicInterior ℝ X))
    (hgrad :
      ⟪grad x, z - x⟫_ℝ =
        ⟪gradientWithin v (intrinsicInterior ℝ X) x, z - x⟫_ℝ)
    (hx : x ∈ intrinsicInterior ℝ X) (hz : z ∈ intrinsicInterior ℝ X) :
    (1 / 2 : ℝ) * ‖x - z‖ ^ 2 ≤ V x z := by
  let s : Set E := intrinsicInterior ℝ X
  let φ : E → ℝ := fun y => v y - (1 / 2 : ℝ) * ‖y‖ ^ 2
  let line : ℝ → E := fun t => AffineMap.lineMap x z t
  have hφconv : ConvexOn ℝ s φ := by
    have hsc : StrongConvexOn s 1 v := by
      simpa [s] using h_strong
    rw [strongConvexOn_iff_convex] at hsc
    simpa [φ] using hsc
  have hline_mem : Set.Icc (0 : ℝ) 1 ⊆ line ⁻¹' s := by
    intro t ht
    have ht0 : 0 ≤ t := ht.1
    have ht1 : t ≤ 1 := ht.2
    have h1t : 0 ≤ 1 - t := sub_nonneg.mpr ht1
    have hsum : 1 - t + t = 1 := by ring
    have hconv : Convex ℝ s := hφconv.1
    have hmem : (1 - t) • x + t • z ∈ s :=
      hconv hx hz h1t ht0 hsum
    simpa [line, AffineMap.lineMap_apply_module] using hmem
  have hφline_conv : ConvexOn ℝ (Set.Icc (0 : ℝ) 1) (fun t => φ (line t)) := by
    simpa [line] using
      (hφconv.comp_affineMap (AffineMap.lineMap x z)).subset hline_mem (convex_Icc 0 1)
  have hmaps : Set.MapsTo line (Set.Icc (0 : ℝ) 1) s := by
    intro t ht
    exact hline_mem ht
  have hline_deriv :
      HasDerivWithinAt line (z - x) (Set.Icc (0 : ℝ) 1) 0 := by
    simpa [line] using
      (AffineMap.hasDerivWithinAt_lineMap (a := x) (b := z)
        (s := Set.Icc (0 : ℝ) 1) (x := (0 : ℝ)))
  have hvdiff : DifferentiableWithinAt ℝ v s x := by
    exact h_diff x hx
  have hvline :
      HasDerivWithinAt (fun t => v (line t))
        ((fderivWithin ℝ v s x) (z - x)) (Set.Icc (0 : ℝ) 1) 0 :=
    hvdiff.hasFDerivWithinAt.comp_hasDerivWithinAt_of_eq 0 hline_deriv hmaps (by simp [line])
  have hnormline :
      HasDerivWithinAt (fun t => ‖line t‖ ^ 2)
        ((2 • innerSL ℝ x) (z - x)) (Set.Icc (0 : ℝ) 1) 0 := by
    have hnormF :
        HasFDerivWithinAt (fun y : E => ‖y‖ ^ 2) (2 • innerSL ℝ x) s x :=
      (hasStrictFDerivAt_norm_sq x).hasFDerivAt.hasFDerivWithinAt
    exact hnormF.comp_hasDerivWithinAt_of_eq 0 hline_deriv hmaps (by simp [line])
  have hφderiv :
      HasDerivWithinAt (fun t => φ (line t))
        ((fderivWithin ℝ v s x) (z - x) -
          (1 / 2 : ℝ) * ((2 • innerSL ℝ x) (z - x)))
        (Set.Icc (0 : ℝ) 1) 0 := by
    simpa [φ] using hvline.sub (HasDerivWithinAt.const_mul (1 / 2 : ℝ) hnormline)
  have hslope :
      (fderivWithin ℝ v s x) (z - x) -
          (1 / 2 : ℝ) * ((2 • innerSL ℝ x) (z - x)) ≤
        slope (fun t => φ (line t)) 0 1 := by
    exact hφline_conv.le_slope_of_hasDerivWithinAt
      (x := (0 : ℝ)) (y := (1 : ℝ))
      (by norm_num) (by norm_num) (by norm_num) hφderiv
  have hgrad_apply :
      (fderivWithin ℝ v s x) (z - x) =
        ⟪gradientWithin v s x, z - x⟫_ℝ := by
    rw [gradientWithin, InnerProductSpace.toDual_symm_apply]
  have hnorm_deriv :
      ((2 • innerSL ℝ x) (z - x)) =
        2 * ⟪x, z - x⟫_ℝ := by
    simp
  have hslope_expanded :
      ⟪gradientWithin v s x, z - x⟫_ℝ -
          ⟪x, z - x⟫_ℝ ≤
        v z - (1 / 2 : ℝ) * ‖z‖ ^ 2 -
          (v x - (1 / 2 : ℝ) * ‖x‖ ^ 2) := by
    have hs := hslope
    rw [hgrad_apply, hnorm_deriv] at hs
    simpa [slope_def_field, φ, line] using hs
  have hnorm_id :
      (1 / 2 : ℝ) * ‖z - x‖ ^ 2 =
        (1 / 2 : ℝ) * ‖z‖ ^ 2 - (1 / 2 : ℝ) * ‖x‖ ^ 2 -
          ⟪x, z - x⟫_ℝ := by
    rw [norm_sub_sq_real, inner_sub_right, real_inner_comm x z,
      real_inner_self_eq_norm_sq]
    ring_nf
  rw [hV, hgrad, norm_sub_rev]
  nlinarith [hslope_expanded, hnorm_id]


/-- Definitional formula for the canonical ambient Bregman divergence.
Layer: Model | Gap: Level 0 (ambient Bregman formula)
Proof: expand the staged Bregman definition.
Source: Lan, First-order and Stochastic Optimization Methods for Machine Learning,
  Eq. (3.2.2).
Used in: stochastic mirror descent Bregman formula bridges
Book citation: `book/FOML/StochasticMirrorDescent.json#/assumptions/5/math`
Origin algorithm: FOML stochastic mirror descent -/
theorem bregmanDivergence_def
    {E : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup E] [InnerProductSpace ℝ E] [CompleteSpace E]
    (v : E → ℝ) (x z : E) :
    bregmanDivergence v x z =
      v z - v x - ⟪∇ v x, z - x⟫_ℝ := by
  rfl

/-- Convexity gives nonnegativity of the canonical ambient Bregman divergence at a
point where the Mathlib gradient realizes the supporting hyperplane.
Layer: Model | Gap: Level 2 (Bregman nonnegativity)
Proof: Restrict `v` to the segment from `x` to `z`, compare the derivative at `0`
  with the secant slope for a convex one-dimensional restriction, and expand.
Source: Lan, First-order and Stochastic Optimization Methods for Machine Learning,
  Proposition 3.1 / Bregman divergence nonnegativity.
Used in: `V_nonneg`, stochastic mirror descent Bregman geometry
Book citation: `book/FOML/StochasticMirrorDescent.json#/assumptions/5/math`
Origin algorithm: FOML stochastic mirror descent -/
theorem bregmanDivergence_nonneg_of_convexOn
    {E : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup E] [InnerProductSpace ℝ E] [CompleteSpace E]
    {X : Set E} {v : E → ℝ} {x z : E}
    (hv : ConvexOn ℝ X v)
    (hx : x ∈ X) (hz : z ∈ X)
    (hgrad : HasGradientAt v (∇ v x) x) :
    0 ≤ bregmanDivergence v x z := by
  unfold bregmanDivergence
  have hsupport : v x + ⟪∇ v x, z - x⟫_ℝ ≤ v z := by
    set g : ℝ → ℝ := fun t => v (AffineMap.lineMap x z t)
    have hline_mem : Set.Icc (0 : ℝ) 1 ⊆ (AffineMap.lineMap x z) ⁻¹' X := by
      intro t ht
      have ht0 : 0 ≤ t := ht.1
      have ht1 : t ≤ 1 := ht.2
      have h1t : 0 ≤ 1 - t := sub_nonneg.mpr ht1
      have hsum : 1 - t + t = 1 := by ring
      have hmem : (1 - t) • x + t • z ∈ X := hv.1 hx hz h1t ht0 hsum
      change AffineMap.lineMap x z t ∈ X
      rw [AffineMap.lineMap_apply_module']
      convert hmem using 1
      module
    have hg_conv : ConvexOn ℝ (Set.Icc (0 : ℝ) 1) g := by
      simpa [g] using
        (hv.comp_affineMap (AffineMap.lineMap x z)).subset hline_mem (convex_Icc 0 1)
    have hg_deriv : HasDerivAt g (⟪∇ v x, z - x⟫_ℝ) 0 := by
      have hg_eq_line : g = fun t : ℝ => v (x + t • (z - x)) := by
        ext t
        simp only [g, AffineMap.lineMap_apply_module']
        congr 1
        module
      rw [hg_eq_line]
      have hfderiv : HasFDerivAt v (InnerProductSpace.toDual ℝ E (∇ v x))
          (x + (0 : ℝ) • (z - x)) := by
        simpa using hgrad.hasFDerivAt
      have hline : HasDerivAt (fun t : ℝ => x + t • (z - x)) ((1 : ℝ) • (z - x)) 0 := by
        exact (hasDerivAt_id 0).smul_const (z - x) |>.const_add x
      have hcomp := hfderiv.comp_hasDerivAt 0 (hline.congr_deriv (one_smul ℝ (z - x)))
      simpa [Function.comp_def, InnerProductSpace.toDual_apply_apply] using hcomp
    have hslope := hg_conv.le_slope_of_hasDerivAt (by norm_num) (by norm_num) zero_lt_one hg_deriv
    simp [g, slope_def_field, AffineMap.lineMap_apply_module'] at hslope
    linarith
  linarith

/-- Strong convexity implies nonnegativity of the canonical ambient Bregman divergence.
Layer: Model | Gap: Level 1 (Bregman nonnegativity)
Proof: Forget the strong-convexity modulus to convexity, then apply the convex
  supporting-hyperplane form.
Source: Lan, First-order and Stochastic Optimization Methods for Machine Learning,
  Proposition 3.1 / Bregman divergence nonnegativity.
Used in: `V_nonneg`, stochastic mirror descent Bregman geometry
Book citation: `book/FOML/StochasticMirrorDescent.json#/assumptions/5/math`
Origin algorithm: FOML stochastic mirror descent -/
theorem bregmanDivergence_nonneg_of_strongConvexOn
    {E : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup E] [InnerProductSpace ℝ E] [CompleteSpace E]
    {X : Set E} {v : E → ℝ} {x z : E}
    (hv : StrongConvexOn X 1 v)
    (hx : x ∈ X) (hz : z ∈ X)
    (hgrad : HasGradientAt v (∇ v x) x) :
    0 ≤ bregmanDivergence v x z := by
  have hconv : ConvexOn ℝ X v := by
    have hv0 : StrongConvexOn X 0 v := hv.mono (by norm_num : (0 : ℝ) ≤ 1)
    simpa using hv0
  exact bregmanDivergence_nonneg_of_convexOn hconv hx hz hgrad

/-- Symmetric half-squared-distance Bregman lower bounds give a squared-distance bound.

If each directed Bregman term lower-bounds one half of the squared norm distance,
then their two-direction sum lower-bounds the full squared norm distance.

Layer: Model | Gap: Level 0 (symmetric Bregman lower bound)
Proof: rewrite the reversed norm with `norm_sub_rev`, transfer the second
  half-bound to the same squared distance, and combine the two real inequalities
  by linear arithmetic.
Source: Mathlib normed additive groups and real linear arithmetic APIs
Used in: stochastic mirror descent conversion from symmetric Bregman control to
  squared-distance control under strong convexity
Book citation: book/FOML/StochasticMirrorDescent.json#/algorithm_spec
Origin algorithm: Lan, First-order and Stochastic Optimization Methods for
  Machine Learning, stochastic mirror descent -/
theorem sq_norm_le_bregman_add_swap_of_half_lower_bound
    {P : Type*} {E : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup E]
    (V : P → P → ℝ) (eval : P → E) (x y : P)
    (hxy : (1 / 2 : ℝ) * ‖eval x - eval y‖ ^ 2 ≤ V x y)
    (hyx : (1 / 2 : ℝ) * ‖eval y - eval x‖ ^ 2 ≤ V y x) :
    ‖eval x - eval y‖ ^ 2 ≤ V x y + V y x := by
  have hnorm : ‖eval y - eval x‖ = ‖eval x - eval y‖ := by
    simpa using norm_sub_rev (eval y) (eval x)
  have hyx' : (1 / 2 : ℝ) * ‖eval x - eval y‖ ^ 2 ≤ V y x := by
    simpa [hnorm] using hyx
  linarith

/-- A continuous real-valued function nonnegative on a dense set is nonnegative everywhere.

Layer: Model | Gap: Level 1 (dense-to-global nonnegativity transfer)
Proof: push the `MapsTo` nonnegativity statement through closure using
  `Set.MapsTo.closure_of_continuousOn`; density puts every point in `closure s`,
  and closedness of `Set.Ici 0` in `ℝ` removes the codomain closure.
Source: Mathlib dense-set closure and closed-order topology APIs
Used in: Bregman divergence nonnegativity on the full feasible carrier from a
  dense model domain
Book citation: book/FOML/StochasticMirrorDescent.json#/algorithm_spec
Origin algorithm: Lan, First-order and Stochastic Optimization Methods for
  Machine Learning, stochastic mirror descent -/
private theorem nonneg_of_continuousOn_of_dense_nonneg_for_model
    {α : Type*} [TopologicalSpace α]
    (D : α → ℝ) (s : Set α)
    (hcont : ContinuousOn D Set.univ)
    (hdense : Dense s)
    (hnonneg : Set.MapsTo D s (Set.Ici (0 : ℝ)))
    (a : α) :
    0 ≤ D a := by
  have hall :
      Set.MapsTo D (closure s) (closure (Set.Ici (0 : ℝ))) :=
    hnonneg.closure_of_continuousOn (by
      exact hcont.mono (by intro y hy; simp))
  have ha : a ∈ closure s := by
    rw [Dense.closure_eq hdense]
    simp
  have h := hall ha
  have hIci_closed : closure (Set.Ici (0 : ℝ)) = Set.Ici 0 := isClosed_Ici.closure_eq
  rw [hIci_closed] at h
  exact h

